WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.270
It's great to welcome back to the program.

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:06.589
Jeff Cohen, who's a journalist and media critic,
founder of fairness and accuracy

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:14.330
in reporting and cofounder of roots action,
which has identified 15 house members that

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:19.190
it considers to be ripe targets for progressive
primary challenges.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:25.490
I think Jeff, for reasons that I'll explain
shortly, my audience will be pleased to hear

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:26.600
me talking about this.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.410
So, I mean, let, let's just talk first about
the, the general principle.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:36.899
In other words, how should we be thinking
of when it makes sense to invest resources

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:37.899
in primaries?

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:40.269
A seat currently held by a Democrat?

00:00:40.269 --> 00:00:44.620
Well, people have to understand that challenging
an incumbent is not easy.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.999
You will generally lose.

00:00:46.999 --> 00:00:56.040
Um, but what we argue in this report is that,
uh, if you run a strong primary challenge

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:03.829
against a corporate democrat or a problematic
Democrat and you don't win, it still helps

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:06.810
you get progressive's organized within that
district.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:13.009
It helps you win elections later and it puts
a scare into the incumbent to start serving

00:01:13.009 --> 00:01:16.979
the interest of the constituents more than
the corporate donors.

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:23.381
So even if it's going to be a seat that ultimately,
uh, stays with the incumbent Democrat, there

00:01:23.381 --> 00:01:29.570
are still specific reasons why it's valuable
to challenge, uh, some many or all of them,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:30.570
no doubt about it.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.479
That a forceful challenge is a success.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:39.799
We've seen this so many places where you don't
knock out the incumbent Congress member, but

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:44.979
some of the activists who've learned electoral
skills, they then become members of the city

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:47.859
council or the county council.

00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:54.109
It really helps build, and I'm not talking
about running 2% protest campaigns, I'm talking

00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:59.439
about getting in the Democratic primary, running
a good campaign, learning the electoral skills.

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:04.340
Even if you don't knock out the incumbent,
it's still worthwhile.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:11.030
So where my audience has some times disagreed
with my position on this is that I've said

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.770
I'm all about moving the Democratic Party
to the left.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.160
I want to look at Senate seats.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.870
I want to look at the House of Representatives.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:24.080
I'm completely with you, but my pragmatic
side also recognizes that in a world of not

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:30.050
unlimited resources and there are some limits
in terms of how much money there is to be

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:35.250
invested in any particular state, district
or seat, there is some calculus to be made

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.310
about okay.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:44.910
If we have the option of trying to primary
one Democrat versus trying to flip to red

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:50.790
seats to blue, that it's okay to make more
specific analyses of particular districts

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:55.830
and states and that sometimes the right answer
might be to leave a relatively centrist Democrat

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:02.920
be for some other goal that may be a higher
or more achievable or productive goal.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:08.120
Is that General Calculus something that you
agree with or disagree with?

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.730
I agree with it up to a point.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:18.170
Obviously progressive's in and their organizations
within a locale need to decide how best to

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:24.650
deploy those resources, but I began and the
whole organization Roots action.org began

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:31.870
in 2011 because we came to this conclusion,
there isn't just one problem facing our country,

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:37.720
which is the impression you get from Msnbc
and at certain news outlets, which is right

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.020
wing extremism.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:45.190
Rightwing republicanism, we believe in equal
problem is that the Democratic Party leadership

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:52.700
is so corporatized and so in effective in
fighting for social, economic, racial, environmental

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:53.700
justice.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:04:00.520
So if you begin with the idea that there are
two problems, sort of equal problems, uh,

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:07.210
then I think you and I would agree that sometimes
it makes sense to go after a Republican and

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:08.820
try to flip it.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:15.410
But a lot of the times it makes sense to go
after the Corporate Democrat because frankly

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:20.769
in these, these bad blues, in this report
we just put out with 15 incumbents, that should

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:25.550
be primaried, 12 of them are in completely
democratic districts.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:33.710
Five of them are in districts where the Democrats
win by 40 50, 70 points when you know Hillary

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.020
versus Trump.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:36.020
Sure.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:43.550
And if you don't primary a bad corporate problematic,
tired Democrat, you really have no congressional

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:47.300
election in your district, it doesn't exist.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.300
That's exactly right.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.300
Yeah.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.360
And I think that you're, it sounds like we
mostly agree.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:53.880
I mean, I sometimes think about West Virginia
where you've got Joe Mansion, arguably the

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.830
most conservative Democrat in the Senate,
and you have three house congressional districts.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:04.270
Uh, it's interesting, the idea of primary
and Joe Mansion at the same time.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:08.740
When I look at, you know, the second district,
which is Alex Mooney's where he won by the

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.810
smallest margin of any of those three.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:12.810
I don't know.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:17.880
I mean, maybe it makes more sense to try to
flip that then to try to primary mansion given

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.630
the full calculus of the state's demographic,
you know, voting, voting demographics and

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:24.630
everything.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:25.630
I don't know.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:28.850
Like, is it crazy to think maybe the resources
are better spent trying to flip one of those

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.910
three congressional districts rather than
fighting mansion?

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:32.980
I'm not sure.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:33.980
Yeah.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:39.610
I'm not an expert on the three districts,
but I'm not arguing with your rationale that

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:42.230
progressive's need to be strategic.

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:46.980
I'm not for like Green party campaigns where
you went 2% of the vote and go, that's not

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.760
what we're talking about.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:53.010
We're talking about running and pour a serious
progressive campaigns.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:06:00.710
Uh, and, uh, in some places where there are
limited resources, you may be right.

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:06.910
Uh, but I have found as I travel around the
country, you know, I do vectors and I bring

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:13.110
out a crowd and there's a lot of activists
that, one of the great frustrations is people

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:19.520
that are saddled sometimes for 10, 20, 30
years with a Democratic Congress member in

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:26.550
a very blue district who's not getting the
job done, who's pro corporate, pro war and

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.330
uh, doesn't stand up to Trump.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:36.290
And in those districts, if you're going to
have any kind of a congressional role as a

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:41.240
constituent, you're going to be running in
the Democratic primary against the incumbent.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.080
So obviously in some districts that won't
make sense.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.370
I would argue in many districts it does make
sense to primary a problematic democrat.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:56.580
So the 15 that you've identified in the report,
is there some common thread in terms of political

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:03.160
